详细说明:
Inclusion Criteria (1)Elderly patients scheduled to undergo gastrointestinal endoscopy; (2)Aged 65-80 years; (3)American Society of Anesthesiologists (ASA) status of Ⅰ-Ⅲ; Exclusion Criteria

1. Declined to participate in the study;
2. Had known allergies or contraindications to benzodiazepines, ciprofol, propofol, or related compounds;
3. Had a history of alcohol abuse or dependence on sedative-hypnotics drugs;
4. Severe cardiovascular diseases, including uncontrolled hypertension(SBP ≥160 mmHg or DBP ≥100 mmHg despite medical treatment) or hypotension (SBP ≤90 mmHg or DBP ≤60 mmHg), resting bradycardia (heart rate \ 30 s; (2)Apnea: cessation of thoracic movement during the procedure, or abruptly flattened PETCO₂ waveform lasting\>15 s; (3)Hypoxemia: SpO₂\ 15 s; (4)Severe hypoxemia: SpO₂ ≤ 85%. Anesthetic Protocol All patients maintained an 8-hour fast and a 4-hour liquid restriction preoperatively, with no preoperative medications administered. Standardized bowel preparation was completed within 24 hours.

After entering the room, the patients were placed in the left lateral position, received oxygen via nasal cannula at 6 L/min, and an upper extremity venous access was established. The non-invasive continuous arterial pressure and cardiac output monitoring system (Model:NICAP-T20A,Hunan Manite Medical Equipment Co., Ltd.) was used for continuous hemodynamic monitoring. A standard NIBP cuff was placed on the left upper arm for regular calibration. An ear clip sensor was attached to the right ear (the long handle end was placed inside the ear cavity close to the ear hole, and the curved end closely fitted the back of the ear), and a toe clip sensor was fixed at the root of the second or third toe of the right foot for continuous measurement. The system was manually calibrated every 1 min using brachial arterial blood pressure as the reference standard. The NICAP system continuously monitored hemodynamic parameters including systolic blood pressure (SBP), diastolic blood pressure (DBP), mean arterial pressure (MAP), heart rate (HR), stroke volume (SV), cardiac output (CO), and systemic vascular resistance (SVR).Ventilation was monitored using a sidestream capnography (Model:KMI605C,Kingst). A pulse oximetry probe was connected, and a transnasal carbon dioxide sampling catheter was placed at the nasal vestibule to continuously sample exhaled gas. Respiratory rate (RR), pulse oxygen saturation (SpO₂), end-tidal carbon dioxide (PETCO₂) were recorded continuously.

Midazolam(Jiangsu Nhwa Pharmaceutical Co., Ltd.) 0.01 mg/kg was injected intravenously first. After the patient's circulation and respiration stabilized, the measured values were recorded as baseline data(T0). Ciprofol(Haisco Pharmaceutical Group Co., Ltd.) or propofol(Xi'an Libang Pharmaceutical Co., Ltd. ) was then infused intravenously at a rate of 10 mL/min. The modified observer's assessment of alertness/sedation (MOAA/S) scale was used to assess sedation depth every 5 seconds. Loss of consciousness was defined as MOAA/S score ≤ 1, at which drug administration was stopped, and the total induction dose of ciprofol or propofol was recorded. SBP, DBP, MAP, HR, SV,CO,SV and SVR were recorded at time points: before drug administration(T0), immediately after loss of consciousness(T1), immediately at endoscope insertion(T2), 1 min(T3), 2 min(T4), 3 min(T5), 4 min(T6) and 5 min(T7) after loss of consciousness. The incidence of respiratory-related adverse event was documented simultaneously. During anesthesia, anesthesiologists administered additional boluses of one-third of the initial induction dose of ciprofol or propofol according to the patient's responses such as eye opening, verbal response and body movement, with an interval of at least 1 min between injections.

During anesthesia, hypotension (SBP \ 2 min) was treated with intravenous ephedrine 6 mg. Bradycardia (HR \ 30 s) was managed with intravenous atropine 0.5 mg. In the event of altered ventilation (RR\ 30 s), apnea (cessation of thoracic movement or abruptly flattened PETCO₂ waveform lasting\>15 s), hypoxemia (SpO₂ \ 15 s), or severe hypoxemia ( SpO₂ ≤ 85%) . Anesthesiologists performed jaw thrust, increased oxygen flow, or provided mask-assisted ventilation; tracheal intubation was performed if necessary.

